Perform a comprehensive "VS Code Marketplace Readiness Review" of this extension. Treat this as if you were reviewing a production extension before it is published to the Visual Studio Marketplace.

Goal:
Polish every aspect of the extension so it looks and feels like a high-quality, professional Marketplace extension.

Review and improve the following:

1. package.json
- Validate all metadata.
- Check name, displayName, description, keywords, categories.
- Ensure publisher, repository, homepage, bugs, license, icon, engines, activationEvents, and contributes are appropriate.
- Improve the short description for discoverability.
- Suggest better keywords for Marketplace search.
- Verify versioning and semantic versioning.

2. README.md
Review the README from the perspective of a Marketplace visitor.

Improve:
- Title
- Introduction
- Features
- Animated demo/GIF placeholders (if missing)
- Screenshots section
- Installation
- Quick Start
- Configuration
- Commands
- Status bar behavior
- Limitations
- FAQ
- Troubleshooting
- Changelog link
- License
- Contributing

Remove ALL development-only or repository-only information.

For example, text like:

"Marketplace Status: This extension is ready for VS Code Marketplace publishing. See MARKETPLACE_READINESS.md..."

must NOT appear in the published README.

The README should be written for end users, not developers.

3. Marketplace Presentation
Review how the extension would appear on the Marketplace.

Evaluate:
- first impression
- professionalism
- clarity
- readability
- discoverability (SEO)
- trustworthiness

Suggest improvements to:
- display name
- description
- feature bullets
- screenshots
- icon
- badges
- branding consistency

4. Extension UX
Review the extension itself.

Check:
- command names
- command titles
- configuration descriptions
- setting names
- status bar text
- notifications
- Quick Pick labels
- error messages
- success messages

Make all user-facing text polished, concise, and consistent.

5. Marketplace Assets
Verify:
- icon resolution and quality
- README image references
- GIF recommendations
- screenshots needed
- LICENSE
- CHANGELOG
- .vscodeignore or files whitelist

Recommend any missing assets.

6. Code Review
Review the extension code for:
- unused code
- dead files
- logging left in production
- TODO/FIXME comments
- debug code
- inefficient APIs
- activation performance
- VS Code API best practices
- error handling

7. Security Review
Check for:
- unsafe filesystem operations
- command injection
- symlink safety
- path traversal
- improper shell usage
- unsafe configuration handling

Recommend improvements where necessary.

8. Marketplace Compliance
Ensure the extension complies with VS Code Marketplace expectations.

Verify:
- package size
- icon
- README
- license
- activation events
- categories
- keywords
- repository links
- publisher metadata

9. Final Polish
Find every piece of text that sounds like it was written for developers instead of users.

Rewrite it in professional, user-friendly language.

Examples include:
- internal notes
- implementation details
- publishing notes
- repository references
- TODOs
- debugging instructions

These should not appear in the published extension.

10. Final Report
Produce a report with four sections:

A. Critical issues (must fix before publishing)
B. Recommended improvements
C. Nice-to-have enhancements
D. Overall Marketplace readiness score (0–100)

For every issue:
- Explain why it matters.
- Show the current content.
- Show the improved content.
- Apply the improvements directly where appropriate.

Do not make superficial changes. Prioritize improvements that enhance user experience, Marketplace discoverability, professionalism, and publish readiness.